+# Private byte-precount / decoder-credit component
+
+This is a finite policy component, not a Peer observation, lock protocol, public factory qualification, decoder-credit release or universal heap theorem. The repository remains clean at b6355270c8e2afffbdeda95430638a2c8fd7a089.
+
+## Real admission and retained lifetime
+
+Before acquiring any body, one original 16 MiB namespace must cover the actual external corpus, every explicitly retained immutable raw, 1,048,577 acquisition bytes (including the real guard byte), 131,072 scratch bytes, 262,144 helper/source metadata bytes, 131,072 ledger bytes, two 65,536-byte root/CAS allowances and 4,456,448 pending stdio/CAS bytes. There is no separate per-reader pool. The component asks for the external live bill again at every guard and reservation; this finite fixture reports its actual input/corpus files. A future public integration must join the actual operation archive's full namespace bill and pending reservation, rather than reuse this fixture bill.
+
+Actual returned bytes enter the strong `unconfirmed` field before type, size, source, callable, current-root or namespace checks. The first error and traceback remain pinned across collection and later attempted reads. An oversize response is reported as an acquired prefix of 1,048,577 bytes, never a complete larger body. No parser or encoder is invoked on it.
+
+The module-level preflight walks actual bytes with a fixed 33-slot stack. It does not build a token list, decoded string list or JSON graph. After reserving the resulting count-based decoder work, the original strict `feature_journeys.contract.json_document` parses the bytes. Its current duplicate-key and nonfinite rejection remain active. Ledger vectors are already booked before calculating entry encoding sizes. Every individual entry obtains an exact ASCII canonical byte count and dictionary-pair count before any encoder call; there is no 8192-byte entry limit. The maximum per-entry encoding workspace is reserved while the whole decoded graph and raw remain charged. The return value contains only the original immutable raw and immutable SHA-256 digest bytes. All granted work remains charged even on success; no graph credit is currently released.
+
+## Explicit count policy and limits of the proof
+
+The decoder work policy is `131072 + 5*rawBytes + 8*quotedWireBytes + 128*stringTokens + 64*primitiveTokens + 2*numericWireBytes + 512*objectPairs + 256*containers + 32*valueTokens` bytes. The terms account for the original bytes plus input Unicode, string/conversion overlap, strict object-pairs-hook tuples/vectors, dictionary resize and scanner memo overlap, scalar conversion and fixed stack/scratch. This is an explicit conservative policy under finite review. Tests establish reservation order and actual custody; they do not establish a universal allocator or parser peak bound. No Python source/header identity is inferred for the locally installed vendor binary.
+
+The closed existing producer keeps at most 512 entries (`feature_journeys/witness.py`, `Journal.publish`: `row['entries'][-512:]`); the component follows that actual producer bound. It imposes no independent 8192-byte per-entry cap. The 32-level finite byte depth is explicitly private preflight policy, not a change to the legacy Client or journal grammar. A future source join must prove the actual closed schema fits it or revise the preflight, without silently rejecting otherwise supported producer data.
+
+The helper source is an actual pinned file/OFD. Its byte source, module-level walker function identities/code/defaults, supplied original callback identities/code/defaults, retained tuple identity, actual root/state identity and fresh on-disk root bytes/OFD are checked. This does not purport to seal the entire stdlib or all arbitrary callback object graphs; those are further public-join obligations.
